From: chromium-rv32
Subject: [PATCH] chromium-rv32 M9: fix RV32 InterpreterEntryTrampoline SEGV by using a real scratch in ResetFeedbackVectorOsrUrgency

Targets V8 src/builtins/riscv/builtins-riscv.cc.

Root cause:
  Generate_InterpreterEntryTrampoline pins a4 to
  kJavaScriptCallDispatchHandleRegister on RV64 but leaves a4 free on
  RV32 (kJavaScriptCallDispatchHandleRegister is no_reg there). The
  per-function RegisterAllocator therefore allocates `feedback_cell` to
  a4 on RV32. The very next call --
    ResetFeedbackVectorOsrUrgency(masm, feedback_vector, a4);
  -- hard-codes a4 as the byte-mask scratch and clobbers feedback_cell.
  Several frames later the trampoline does
    __ Lw(scratch, FieldMemOperand(feedback_cell,
                                   offsetof(FeedbackCell, interrupt_budget_)));
  and faults at libv8.so+<symbol>+0x118 with SEGV_MAPERR @ 0x8 every
  time a user function exhausts its interrupt budget (~10 invocations,
  reproducible with Array.from({length:10}, (_,i)=>i)).

Fix:
  Replace the hard-coded a4 with a macro-assembler scratch obtained via
  UseScratchRegisterScope, mirroring the upstream pattern used at the
  second ResetFeedbackVectorOsrUrgency call site higher up in this
  file. The scratch comes from the masm's reserved scratch pool, which
  is disjoint from the per-function RegisterAllocator pool, so it can
  never alias feedback_cell on any architecture.

Verified by:
  * milestones/m6-svelte-in-browser/probe/d8-triggers/ -- all 10
    qemu-user d8 reproducers (T1..T10) PASS without the path-A
    workaround.
  * milestones/m9-v8-correctness -- full Chromium content_shell run
    inside qemu-system-riscv32 against the Svelte 5 hydration demo
    captures four m6:* sentinels + m6:all-green + a rendered four-light
    PNG (artifacts/m6-screenshot-20260522-022142.png) with zero
    InterpreterEntryTrampoline SEGV markers in the log.

This single change replaces the M6 path-A workaround that had disabled
the interrupt-budget mechanism entirely on RV32. With this fix
applied, JS functions are once again allowed to tier up via the normal
budget-interrupt path.

diff --git a/src/builtins/riscv/builtins-riscv.cc b/src/builtins/riscv/builtins-riscv.cc
--- a/src/builtins/riscv/builtins-riscv.cc
+++ b/src/builtins/riscv/builtins-riscv.cc
@@ -1339,7 +1339,22 @@ void Builtins::Generate_InterpreterEntryTrampoline(
                                 &push_stack_frame);
 
 #ifndef V8_JITLESS
-  ResetFeedbackVectorOsrUrgency(masm, feedback_vector, a4);
+  // chromium-rv32 M9 fix: upstream passes `a4` as the OSR-urgency-reset
+  // scratch. On RV64 that is safe because `a4` is pinned to
+  // kJavaScriptCallDispatchHandleRegister and `feedback_cell` is therefore
+  // never allocated to `a4`. On RV32 dispatch_handle is `no_reg`, `a4` is
+  // unpinned, and the register allocator hands `a4` to `feedback_cell` --
+  // the same register the upstream code then clobbers as a temp. The
+  // post-frame-setup `Lw scratch, FieldMemOperand(feedback_cell, ...)`
+  // then dereferences a garbage pointer and SEGVs at the start of the
+  // "Reduce interrupt budget" sequence (M6 path-A repro). Use a proper
+  // macro-assembler scratch register here, mirroring the pattern used at
+  // the second ResetFeedbackVectorOsrUrgency call site higher up in this
+  // file. See milestones/m9-v8-correctness/STATUS.md.
+  {
+    UseScratchRegisterScope temps(masm);
+    ResetFeedbackVectorOsrUrgency(masm, feedback_vector, temps.Acquire());
+  }
 
   // Increment invocation count for the function.
   DEFINE_PINNED(count, a6);
